<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Cavendish</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1731">1731</a>–<a href="/years/1810">1810</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Cavendish was an English experimental and theoretical chemist and physicist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, a grandson of William Cavendish, 2nd Duke of Devonshire, who was known for his reclusive personality and the precision of his research. In a 1766 paper, On Factitious Airs, he described hydrogen, which he called inflammable air, and its forming water on combustion; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoine-lavoisier">Antoine Lavoisier</a> later gave the element its name. In 1797–1798 at Clapham Common he used a torsion balance built by John Michell to measure the density of the Earth, finding it 5.48 times that of water, in what is now called the Cavendish experiment. He died one of the wealthiest men in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-cavendish.jpg" alt="Henry Cavendish" width="330" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Wilson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cavendish_Henry_signature.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
